# Lockfiles are generated. Their contents are the point, not their formatting, so git
# should never try to merge or diff them: a reviewer wants to see "the lockfile changed",
# and `linguist-generated` keeps them out of language statistics and off the diff of a
# routine dependency bump.

# Pixi: the conda solve. Binary because two solvers can produce different orderings for the
# same package set, and a textual merge of that is a corrupt lockfile rather than a conflict.
pixi.lock merge=binary linguist-generated=true -diff

# Cargo: the crate graph. Same reasoning — `cargo update` rewrites the whole file, and a
# line-level merge produces a `Cargo.lock` that no longer matches any manifest.
Cargo.lock merge=binary linguist-generated=true -diff

# Bun: the JS workspace resolution. Textual, unlike the other two, because bun rewrites only
# the entries it changed and a textual merge is usually correct. Kept out of statistics
# regardless.
bun.lock linguist-generated=true

# ── source defaults ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Everything is UTF-8 text unless a file says otherwise, so declare it once here rather
# than trusting the absence of binary content. `-text` is not set: the lockfiles above opt
# out individually, and a blanket `* text=auto eol=lf` is what makes a stray PNG render as
# a diff in a pull request.
* text=auto

# Real binary assets, which `text=auto` cannot infer reliably from a file with no
# extension or an unusual one.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.webp binary
*.gif binary
*.ico binary
*.tif binary
*.tiff binary
*.glb binary
*.gltf binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary

# Shell scripts must be LF. A CRLF shebang is not a warning, it is a file that cannot run,
# and the failure appears on a Linux CI runner that checked out the file nobody tested on
# Windows. `.bat` and `.cmd` are the deliberate exception: they need CRLF.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
scripts/*.ps1 text eol=crlf
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Languages where the line ending is not noise. A `.ps1` that a Linux runner has to run is a
# `.ps1`; a `.ps1` in a docs snippet is not. The one that matters is scripts/.

# Documentation: markdown is prose, and trailing whitespace in it is a hard line break that
# someone is sometimes relying on, so no whitespace rule here either.
*.md text
